
  riscvsim › demo 4 · Guessing wrong
  A branch guesses, misses, and wrong work is thrown away.

   1  # The pipeline guesses where a branch goes before it
   2  # knows. A wrong guess throws away what came in behind it.
   3  addi x5, x0, 3     # run the loop 3 times
   4  addi x6, x0, 0
   5  loop:
   6  addi x6, x6, 1     # count each pass
   7  addi x5, x5, -1
   8  bne  x5, x0, loop  # guessed, then checked in EX
   9  addi x7, x0, 99    # after the loop

  cycle 1
    IF addi x5, x0, 3  ID ·  EX ·  MEM ·  WB ·
    › The first instruction, addi, is fetched from memory.

  cycle 2
    IF addi x6, x0, 0  ID addi x5, x0, 3  EX ·  MEM ·  WB ·
    › addi x5 is decoded and reads its source registers.

  cycle 3
    IF addi x6, x6, 1  ID addi x6, x0, 0  EX addi x5, x0, 3  MEM ·  WB ·
    › addi x5 is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 4
    IF addi x5,x5,-1  ID addi x6,x6,1  EX addi x6,x0,0  MEM addi x5,x0,3  WB ·
    › addi x6 is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 5
    IF bne x5,x0…  ID addi x5,x5…  EX addi x6,x6…  MEM addi x6…  WB addi x5…
    ✓ x5 = 3
    › addi x6, x6, 1 needs x6 from addi x6, x0, 0, so it is forwarded from the
      EX/MEM pipeline register and nobody waits.
      Forwarding: a result goes straight to the instruction that needs it,
      before it is written to its register.
      A pipeline register holds an instruction and its values between two
      stages.

  cycle 6
    IF addi x7,x0…  ID bne x5,x0…  EX addi x5,x5…  MEM addi x6…  WB addi x6…
    › addi finishes and writes x6 = 0.

  cycle 7
    IF ·  ID ✗ addi x7,x0…  EX bne x5,x0…  MEM addi x5,x5,-1  WB addi x6,x6,1
    ✓ x6 = 1
    ✗ The branch was wrong (bne was guessed not taken, but it was taken), so
      one instruction was thrown away.
      A flush: instructions fetched down the wrong path are removed before
      they change anything.

  cycle 8
    IF addi x6,x6,1  ID flushed  EX flushed  MEM bne x5,x0…  WB addi x5,x5,-1
    ✓ x5 = 2
    › addi finishes and writes x5 = 2.

  cycle 9
    IF addi x5,x5,-1  ID addi x6,x6,1  EX flushed  MEM flushed  WB bne x5,x0…
    › addi x6 is decoded and reads its source registers.

  cycle 10
    IF bne x5,x0…  ID addi x5,x5,-1  EX addi x6,x6,1  MEM flushed  WB flushed
    › addi x6 is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 11
    IF addi x6,x6…  ID bne x5,x0…  EX addi x5,x5…  MEM addi x6,x6…  WB flushed
    › addi x5 is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 12
    IF addi x5,x5…  ID addi x6,x6…  EX bne x5,x0…  MEM addi x5…  WB addi x6…
    ✓ x6 = 2
    › bne needs x5 from addi x5, so it is forwarded from the EX/MEM pipeline
      register and nobody waits.

  cycle 13
    IF bne x5,x0…  ID addi x5,x5…  EX addi x6,x6…  MEM bne x5,x0…  WB addi x5…
    ✓ x5 = 1
    › addi finishes and writes x5 = 1.

  cycle 14
    IF addi x6,x6…  ID bne x5,x0…  EX addi x5,x5…  MEM addi x6…  WB bne x5,x0…
    › addi x5 is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 15
    IF ✗ addi x5…  ID ✗ addi x6…  EX bne x5,x0…  MEM addi x5,x5…  WB addi x6…
    ✓ x6 = 3
    ✗ The branch was wrong (bne was guessed taken, but it fell through), so
      two instructions were thrown away.

  cycle 16
    IF addi x7,x0,99  ID flushed  EX flushed  MEM bne x5,x0…  WB addi x5,x5,-1
    ✓ x5 = 0
    › addi finishes and writes x5 = 0.

  cycle 17
    IF ·  ID addi x7, x0, 99  EX flushed  MEM flushed  WB bne x5, x0, loop
    › addi is decoded and reads its source registers.

  cycle 18
    IF ·  ID ·  EX addi x7, x0, 99  MEM flushed  WB flushed
    › addi is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 19
    IF ·  ID ·  EX ·  MEM addi x7, x0, 99  WB flushed
    › Nothing is left to fetch, so the pipeline is emptying.

  cycle 20
    IF ·  ID ·  EX ·  MEM ·  WB addi x7, x0, 99
    ✓ x7 = 99
    ✓ addi writes x7 = 99, and the program is finished.

  ✓ 20 cycles · 12 instructions · CPI 1.67 · 0 stalls · 2 mispredictions
    Ends with x5 = 0 · x6 = 3 · x7 = 99
    Why it slowed down:
    • The branch was wrong (bne was guessed not taken, but it was taken), so
      one instruction was thrown away.
    • The branch was wrong (bne was guessed taken, but it fell through), so
      two instructions were thrown away.

